  "query_preview": "..\n(Thursday through next Wednesday)\nIssued at 1044 PM CDT WED AUG 12 2026\n\nLatest water vapor imagery reveals an upper level ridge building\nout to our west with a shortwave moving along the eastern \nperiphery of the ridge, which is helping push some showers and \nstorms across the Tennessee Valley this evening. At the surface, \nhigh pressure is currently centered across the Northern Gulf. This\nfeature will likely remain anchored across the Gulf through the \nwork week with a slight jaunt to the north over the weekend. The \nupper level ridge will expand and strengthen through the week, \nallowing for temps to steadily warm up through the week with highs\neventually settling into the mid to upper 90s for most of Central\nAlabama. Continued humid conditions combined with these temps \nwill bump heat indices into the 105-110 degree range. The latest \nECMWF EFI hints at unusually warm temperatures for us over the \nweekend, generally 10 degrees above normal for mid August. This \nwill lead to a major to extreme heat risk for Central Alabama late\nthis week and over the weekend. Heat Advisories are currently in \neffect for Thursday and Friday. These will likely be extended \nthrough the weekend.\n\nThis pattern will promote a typically summertime convection\nregime with low to moderate (20-50%) chances for showers and\nstorms. Greatest chances for showers and storms will be across\nour southern areas as deeper moisture spreads inland. Those\nacross central and north-central Alabama will likely remain dry\nas ridging helps keep a lid on things.\n\nOtherwise, fog potential is low tonight. RAP guidance hints at\nnarrowing dew points and a few pockets of lowering condensation\npressure deficits. This would lead to some patchy fog, generally\nfor those that saw some rain earlier today.\n\n95/Castillo",
